  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q16-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "16",
    "prompt": "Let $n$ be a positive integer. Suppose we write down the $n$ consecutive numbers immediately after 999, and write down the $n$ consecutive numbers immediately before 999. The sum of those after 999 is twice the sum of those before 999. Find $n$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n=665$.\n\nUse $1+2+\\cdots+n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}$ to express the two sums, then equate the first to twice the second.",
      "worked": "Let $A$ be the sum after 999 and $B$ the sum before 999.\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=999n+\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\\\B&=999n-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $A=2B$,\n$$\\begin{align*}999n+\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}&=2\\left(999n-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\right)\\\\999n+\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}&=1998n-n(n+1)\\\\\\frac{3n(n+1)}{2}&=999n\\\\3n(n+1)&=1998n\\\\n(n+1)&=666n.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $n>0$, divide by $n$:\n$$\\begin{align*}n+1&=666\\\\n&=665.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
